# Groovy Style

> Distilled from the internal *Groovy Coding Standard* — only the formats marked
> recommended (√ / green) are captured here. See also [groovy-implicit-return.mdc](mdc:groovy-implicit-return.mdc)
> for the implicit-return rule (never add explicit `return` to satisfy a linter).

Reference: [Apache Groovy Style Guide](https://groovy-lang.org/style-guide.html).

Bracket convention matches the marslo Bash/Python rules: **single inner space on
each side of the outermost bracket pair; nested inner pairs stay tight.**

## 1. Trailing space

Never leave trailing whitespace — configure the editor to strip it automatically.

## 2. Curly braces (K&R / one true brace)

Opening `{` stays on the same line as the statement; never on its own line.

```groovy
if ( condition ) {          // ✅
  ...
}

if ( condition )            // ❌ Allman — brace on its own line
{
  ..
}
```

## 3. Shebang

```groovy
#!/usr/bin/env groovy       // ✅ portable
#!/usr/bin/groovy           // ❌ hardcoded path
#!/usr/local/bin/groovy     // ❌ hardcoded path
```

## 4. Groovydoc comment

Open `/**`, continuation ` * `, close ` */` or with `**/`.

```groovy
/**                         // ✅ close with */
 * ...
 */
```
```groovy
/**                         // ✅ closes with **/
 * ...
**/
```

Description block (everything before the first `@param`) — use **HTML tags**
(`<code>`, `<p>`, `<pre>`, `<ul>`, `<a>` …), not Groovydoc `{@code}` / `{@link}`.
LSPs render HTML but cannot parse the `{@...}` javadoc signs, so `{@code}` in the
description shows up as raw text on hover. Keep `{@code}` / `{@link}` only inside
the `@param` / `@return` / `@see` tag lines, and align the tags.

```groovy
/**
 * find the matches tags for <code>revision</code> with default pattern matches.
 * <p>
 * <pre><code>
 *   tagMatches( 'v1.*', 'HEAD', '.' )
 * </code></pre>
 *
 * @param pattern   the pattern to match the tag(s) for {@code revision}
 * @param revision  the revision to fetch tag(s). using {@code HEAD} by default
 * @param dir       the local repo location. using current directory by default
 * @return          find the matches list or empty ArrayList
 * @see             {@link util#capture(java.lang.String, java.lang.String, int, int)}
**/
```

## 5. Whitespace

One space around binary and ternary operators:

```
=   +   -   <   >   *   /   %   |   &   ^   <=   >=   ==   !=   ?   :
```

Single inner space for the outermost bracket; nested pairs tight:

```groovy
for ( int i = 0; i < 10; i++ )                      // ✅
for(int i = 0; i < 10; i++)                         // ❌

if ( condition )                                    // ✅
if(condition)                                       // ❌

void function( String str, Boolean boolean )        // ✅
void function(String str,Boolean b)                 // ❌
void function( String str, Boolean boolean, pwd() ) // ✅ inner pwd() tight

List list = [ 'a', 'b', 'c' ]                       // ✅
List list=['a','b','c']                             // ❌

List list = [ 'a', 'b', ['1', '2'] ]                // ✅ outer padded, nested [ ] tight
```

Multi-line condition — align continuation, put `) {` on its own line:

```groovy
if ( (condition1 && condition2) ||
     (condition3 && condition4) ||
     ! (condition5 && condition6)
) {
  ...
}
```

## 6. Quotes

- single quotes — string **without** interpolation
- double quotes — **interpolated** string

```groovy
String str = 'this string has no variable'
String str = "the variable is : ${variable}"
```

## 7. `==` — literal on the left (Yoda)

Put the hardcoded value on the left so a typo can't assign the variable.

```groovy
'1' == str                    // ✅ safety
str == '1'                    // ❌ risky: `str = '1'` typo silently assigns
```

## 8. `def` & type

Declare the explicit type; reserve `def` for genuinely dynamic values.

```groovy
String name = "Guillaume"     // ✅ recommended
def name = "Guillaume"        // ❌ not recommended
```

## 9. Wrapping lines

### String (triple-quote)

Closing `'''` on its own line — never appended to the last content line.

```groovy
String str = '''            // ✅
               line 1
               line 2
               line 3
             '''

String str = '''            // ❌ closing quotes glued to line 3
               line 3'''
```

### List

Opening `[` on the declaration line, elements aligned, closing `]` on its own line:

```groovy
List<String> recipients = [ 'sjc@example.edu',
                            'dan@example.com',
                            'spm@example.edu',
                            'jim@example.org'
                          ]

List<String> recipients = [           // ✅ also acceptable
                            'sjc@example.edu',
                            'dan@example.com',
                          ]
```

### function

Wrap args aligned; put `) {` on its own line.

```groovy
def someMethod( int anArg,
                Object anotherArg,
                String yetAnotherArg,
                Object andStillAnother
) {
  ...
}

def someMethod (            // ✅ also acceptable
  int anArg,
  Object anotherArg,
) {
  ...
}
```

Long expression argument — align continuations, closing `)` on its own line:

```groovy
throw new PluginException( "Failed to execute command list-applications:" +
                           " The group with name " +
                           parameterMap.groupname[0] +
                           " is not compatible group of type " +
                           SERVER_TYPE_NAME
                         )
```

## 10. if

```groovy
if ( condition ) {          // ✅
...
}
if(condition){              // ❌
  ...
}

if ( condition1 &&
     condition2 &&
     ! condition3
) {
  ...
}
```

## 11. try / catch / finally

```groovy
try {
  ...
} catch {
  ...
} finally {
  ...
}
```

## 12. switch / case

Space the `switch ( ... )` parens; `case X :` with a space before the colon.

```groovy
switch ( string ) {
  case 'foo' :
    result = "string"
    break
  case [ 'a', 'b', 'c' ] :
    result = "list"
    break
  case Integer :
    result = "integer"
    break
  default :
    result = 'default'
}
```

## 13. Method chaining

Break before each `.`, align the chain under the first call.

```groovy
List foo = Jenkins.instance.getAllItems(...)
                           .findAll{ ... }
                           .collect { ... }

List foo = map.findAll{
                ...
              }
              .unique()
              .sort()
              .collect {
                ...
              }
```

## 14. Syntactic sugar

Prefer the Groovy shorthand:

| construct | ✅ use | ❌ instead of |
|---|---|---|
| ternary if | `a ? b : c` | `if (a) b else c` |
| elvis | `a ?: b` | `a ? a : b` |
| null safe | `a?.b` | `( a == null ) ? a : a.b` |
| implicit return | `return i > 1` | `if ( i > 1 ) { return true } else { return false }` |
| groovy truth | `if ( name ) { .. }` | `if ( name != null && name.length > 0 ) { .. }` |
